    "prediction": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The reaction of propene with benzene to produce isopropylbenzene is a Friedel-Crafts alkylation catalyzed by an acid. Aqueous hydrobromic acid contains water, which acts as a nucleophile and competes with the benzene, leading to the formation of isopropyl alcohol as a major side product. Anhydrous liquid hydrogen fluoride acts as a strong, non-nucleophilic acid catalyst that promotes the formation of the isopropyl cation without providing water, making it more selective for the desired alkylation.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
