    "prediction": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To compute the integral $I=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{(x^2 + 1)^2} dx$, we use the trigonometric substitution $x = \\tan(\\theta)$, which implies $dx = \\sec^2(\\theta) d\\theta$. The integral transforms to $\\int_{-\\pi/2}^{\\pi/2} \\frac{\\sec^2(\\theta)}{\\sec^4(\\theta)} d\\theta = \\int_{-\\pi/2}^{\\pi/2} \\cos^2(\\theta) d\\theta$, which evaluates to $\\int_{-\\pi/2}^{\\pi/2} \\frac{1 + \\cos(2\\theta)}{2} d\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$. Since $\\pi \\approx 3.14159$, the value $\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\approx 1.57$, which corresponds to option G.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
