    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "For independent and identically distributed random variables $X_n$ with an exponential distribution $Exp(1)$, the tail probability is $P(X_n > x) = e^{-x}$. Using the Borel-Cantelli lemmas, one can show that for any $\\epsilon > 0$, the sum $\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} P(X_n > (1+\\epsilon)\\log n) = \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} n^{-(1+\\epsilon)}$ converges, while $\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} P(X_n > (1-\\epsilon)\\log n) = \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} n^{-(1-\\epsilon)}$ diverges. Consequently, the limit superior $\\limsup_{n \\rightarrow \\infty} \\frac{X_n}{\\log n}$ is equal to 1 with probability 1.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
