    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_6921",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What is the value of the series $\\sum_{{k=1}}^{{\\infty}} \\frac{{(-1)^{{k-1}}{{k}} \\sum_{{n=0}}^{{\\infty}} \\frac{{1}}{{k 2^n+1}}$?\n\nOptions: \nA. $\\frac{{\\pi}}{{2}}$\nB. 2.0\nC. $\\ln(2)$\nD. $\\pi$\nE. 1.0\nF. $\\sqrt{{2}}$\nG. 0.5\nH. $\\frac{{\\sqrt{{3}}{{2}}$\nI. 0.0\nJ. 1.5\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The series is interpreted as \\(\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{(-1)^{k-1}}{k} \\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{k2^n+1}\\). Using the integral representation \\(\\frac{1}{k2^n+1}=\\int_0^1 x^{k2^n}dx\\), swapping sums and integral, and applying the identity \\(\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{(-1)^{k-1}}{k}x^{k2^n}=\\ln(1+x^{2^n})\\), the double sum becomes \\(\\int_0^1\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\ln(1+x^{2^n})dx\\). The infinite product \\(\\prod_{n=0}^{\\infty}(1+x^{2^n})=\\frac{1}{1-x}\\) for \\(|x|<1\\) gives \\(\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\ln(1+x^{2^n})=-\\ln(1-x)\\), so the integral evaluates to \\(\\int_0^1-\\ln(1-x)dx=1\\). Thus the value is \\(1\\), corresponding to option E.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
