    "global index": "MMLUPro_health_4873",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A problem for immune system theories of senescence is\n\nOptions: \nA. Determining what first causes the senescence in the immune system\nB. The immune system theories do not explain the role of lifestyle in senescence\nC. Immune system theories do not explain the role of genetic factors in senescence\nD. Immune system theories are too complex to test effectively\nE. The lack of information about senescence in general\nF. That the immune system improves with age\nG. Immune system theories cannot account for the environmental factors in senescence\nH. That the immune system does not decline with age\nI. The difficulty of isolating the immune system from other bodily systems\nJ. Auto-immune disorders are far too rare\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Immune system theories of senescence, often called \"immunosenescence,\" generally argue that the gradual decline of immune function leads to aging. However, a major theoretical problem is the \"chicken-or-egg\" dilemma: it remains difficult to determine whether the immune system's decline causes aging, or if it is merely a secondary consequence of the aging process itself. Therefore, the primary challenge is determining what initiates this senescence.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
