    "global index": "MMLUPro_health_5524",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A new severe respiratory illness caused by a newly identified virus is discovered. Which of the following properties of a killed vaccine relative to a live vaccine is the most appropriate rationale for developing a killed vaccine for this illness?\n\nOptions: \nA. Is more likely to provide sterilizing immunity\nB. Can be administered in higher doses\nC. Develops more rapid protective immunity\nD. Is less likely to require subsequent boosters for lifelong immunity\nE. Is less likely to cause an allergic reaction\nF. Is more effective for immunocompromised individuals\nG. Is most likely to generate mucosal immunity\nH. Is less likely to cause local adverse reactions at the site of injection\nI. Avoids the concern for reversion to virulence\nJ. Is less likely to be affected by preexisting antibodies\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Live-attenuated vaccines carry the small but significant risk of mutating back into a pathogenic form (reversion to virulence), which is a major safety concern when developing a vaccine for a new, severe illness. Killed (inactivated) vaccines contain pathogens that have been completely inactivated and thus cannot replicate or cause the disease they are meant to prevent. Therefore, avoiding the risk of reversion to virulence is the primary safety rationale for preferring an inactivated approach.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
