    "global index": "MMLUPro_health_5397",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A study is conducted to assess the effectiveness of a new drug for the treatment of type 2 diabetes mellitus. A total of 1000 patients with type 2 diabetes mellitus are enrolled. Patients are randomly assigned to receive the new drug or standard treatment. The alpha and beta values for calculating probability are 0.05 and 0.20, respectively. Results show that the new drug is significantly better than standard treatment. If this study had been performed in a population of only 500 patients, which of the following would have been most likely to increase?\n\nOptions: \nA. Chance of a type II error\nB. Chance of a false positive result\nC. Size of the study population\nD. Power of the study\nE. Sensitivity of the study\nF. Specificity of the study\nG. Chance of a false negative result\nH. Probability of the alpha value\nI. Chance of a type I error\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Decreasing the sample size of a study reduces its power (the probability of correctly rejecting a false null hypothesis). Since power is defined as $1 - \\beta$ (where $\\beta$ is the probability of a type II error), a decrease in power directly results in an increase in the probability of committing a type II error ($\\beta$). A type II error is synonymous with a false negative result, meaning both options A and G describe the same statistical outcome, with \"type II error\" being the standard technical term.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
      "success": true,
