    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A 22 years old male presented with history of fever, sore throat and enlarged neck lymph nodes. He was ordered a Paul-Bunnell test with a suspicion of Infectious mononucleosis. What is the immunological basis behind the use of this test?\n\nOptions: \nA. Heterophile antibody test\nB. Complement mediated agglutination reaction\nC. Homophile antibody test\nD. Latex agglutination test\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The Paul-Bunnell test is used to detect the presence of heterophile antibodies in the patient's serum, which are characteristic of Epstein-Barr virus infection. These antibodies are called heterophile because they react with antigens from a different species, specifically sheep or horse erythrocytes, causing agglutination. Therefore, the immunological basis of the test is the detection of heterophile antibodies.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
