  {
    "global index": "MedMCQA_337",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Induction of inhalational agent is faster.\n\nOptions: \nA. Agent with high blood gas solubility\nB. Combined with nitrous oxide\nC. Person with increased residual volume\nD. Right to left shunt\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The second gas effect occurs when a high concentration of nitrous oxide is absorbed, concentrating the remaining anesthetic agent in the alveoli and accelerating its uptake. Conversely, high blood-gas solubility, increased residual volume, and right-to-left shunts all act to slow the induction of inhalational anesthesia. Therefore, combining an agent with nitrous oxide is the correct method to achieve a faster induction.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
