    "global index": "MedMCQA_2513",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: When broad-spectrum antibiotics are administered with coumarin anticoagulants, the anticoagulant action may be:\n\nOptions: \nA. Reduced because of enhanced hepatic drug metabolism\nB. Reduced because of increased protein-binding\nC. Increased because of reduction of vitamin 'K' sources\nD. Increased because of decreased renal excretion of the anticoagulant\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Broad-spectrum antibiotics can reduce the population of intestinal flora, which are responsible for synthesizing a portion of the body's vitamin K supply. Since vitamin K is required for the synthesis of clotting factors that coumarin anticoagulants inhibit, a reduction in vitamin K levels enhances the anticoagulant effect. Therefore, the administration of these antibiotics can potentiate the action of coumarin anticoagulants.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
      "success": true,
