    "global index": "MusicTheoryBench_172",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following harmonic intervals has an incorrect structure?\n\nOptions: \nA. Major third: L:1/4\nM:none\nK:C\n [B,^e]4 |] %1\nB. Diminished fifth: L:1/4\nM:none\nK:C\n [^^F^B]4 |] %1\nC. Minor seventh: L:1/4\nM:none\nK:C\n [_Da]4 |] %1\nD. Diminished sixth: L:1/4\nM:none\nK:C\n [^Gd]4 |] %1\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let's analyze each interval based on standard interval structures in music theory:\n\n- A. [B,^e]: B to E is a perfect fourth, but raised E (^e) makes it an augmented fourth, not a major third. So this is incorrect.\n- B. [^^F^B]: Double-sharpened F is G, and ^B is B♯; G to B♯ is an augmented third, not a diminished fifth—also incorrect.\n- C. [_Da]: D to A is a perfect fifth; lowered D (_D) to A is still a fifth, but A is not lowered, so _D to A is an augmented fifth, not a minor seventh—this is wrong too.\n- D. [^Gd]: G♯ to D is a diminished fifth (tritone), not a diminished sixth. A diminished sixth would require a sixth, like G♯ to E♭♭. So this interval is mislabeled.\n\nAll options have issues, but D is the most clearly incorrect in structure because ^G to d is a diminished fifth, not a diminished sixth. The label \"diminished sixth\" is wrong.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 209,
        "output_tokens": 241,
        "total_tokens": 450
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
