    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4489",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This question refers to the following information.\n\"Those whose condition is such that their function is the use of their bodies and nothing better can be expected of them, those, I say, are slaves of nature. It is better for them to be ruled thus.\"\nJuan de Sepulveda, Politics, 1522\n\"When Latin American nations gained independence in the 19th century, those two strains converged, and merged with an older, more universalist, natural law tradition. The result was a distinctively Latin American form of rights discourse. Paolo Carozza traces the roots of that discourse to a distinctive application, and extension, of Thomistic moral philosophy to the injustices of Spanish conquests in the New World. The key figure in that development seems to have been Bartolomé de Las Casas, a 16th-century Spanish bishop who condemned slavery and championed the cause of Indians on the basis of a natural right to liberty grounded in their membership in a single common humanity. 'All the peoples of the world are humans,' Las Casas wrote, and 'all the races of humankind are one.' According to Brian Tierney, Las Casas and other Spanish Dominican philosophers laid the groundwork for a doctrine of natural rights that was independent of religious revelation 'by drawing on a juridical tradition that derived natural rights and natural law from human rationality and free will, and by appealing to Aristotelian philosophy.'\"\nMary Ann Glendon, \"The Forgotten Crucible: The Latin American Influence on the Universal Human Rights Idea,” 2003\nWhich of the following presidents was most involved in Latin American politics in the twentieth century?\n\nOptions: \nA. Theodore Roosevelt\nB. Franklin D. Roosevelt\nC. Harry S. Truman\nD. Chester Arthur\nE. Dwight Eisenhower\nF. Woodrow Wilson\nG. James K. Polk\nH. James Monroe\nI. John F. Kennedy\nJ. Richard Nixon\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "While several presidents in this list were involved in Latin American affairs during the twentieth century, Theodore Roosevelt is most famously associated with assertive interventionist policies such as the \"Big Stick\" ideology and the Roosevelt Corollary to the Monroe Doctrine. However, if forced to choose based on the general historical prominence of 20th-century involvement, Theodore Roosevelt, Franklin D. Roosevelt, and John F. Kennedy are all strong candidates; Theodore Roosevelt's actions in the early 20th century, particularly regarding the Panama Canal and the Roosevelt Corollary, fundamentally redefined U.S.-Latin American relations. Given the common historical emphasis on his specific policy shifts, A is the most frequent answer in this context.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 466,
        "output_tokens": 282,
        "total_tokens": 748
      },
      "provider": "vertex",
      "error": null
    },
    "accuracy": 1.0,
    "cost": 0.0005395
  },
  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4592",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This question refers to the following information.\n\"The challenge of the next half century is whether we have the wisdom to use wealth to enrich and elevate our national life, and to advance the quality of our American civilization….The Great Society rests on abundance and liberty for all. It demands an end to poverty and racial injustice, to which we are totally committed in our time. But that is just the beginning. The Great Society is a place where every child can find knowledge to enrich his mind and to enlarge his talents. It is a place where leisure is a welcome chance to build and reflect, not a feared cause of boredom and restlessness. It is a place where the city of man serves not only the needs of the body and the demands of commerce but the desire for beauty and the hunger for community. It is a place where man can renew contact with nature. It is a place which honors creation for its own sake and for what it adds to the understanding of the race. It is a place where men are more concerned with the quality of their goals than the quantity of their goods. But most of all, the Great Society is not a safe harbor, a resting place, a final objective, a finished work. It is a challenge constantly renewed, beckoning us toward a destiny where the meaning of our lives matches the marvelous products of our labor.\"\nLyndon Johnson, Remarks at the University of Michigan, Ann Arbor, 1964\nAlong with his goals of establishing a Great Society, Johnson was also engaged in which of the following initiatives?\n\nOptions: \nA. Undermining Communism in Korea with the Korean War\nB. Undermining Communism in Russia during the Cuban Missile Crisis\nC. Undermining Communism in China with the Cultural Revolution\nD. Undermining Communism in Afghanistan with the Soviet-Afghan War\nE. Undermining Communism in East Germany with the Berlin Airlift\nF. Undermining Communism in the Eastern Block by demanding a removal of the Berlin Wall\nG. Undermining Communism in Cuba with the Bay of Pigs Invasion\nH. Undermining Communism in Laos with the Laotian Civil War\nI. Undermining Communism in Turkey and Greece using economic aid\nJ. Undermining Communism in Vietnam after the Tet Offensive\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
