    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4724",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What do anthropologists NOT need to know to determine when people entered the Americas from Siberia?\n\nOptions: \nA. When sled dogs were first domesticated\nB. When eastern Siberia was first inhabited\nC. When the Bering Strait was exposed and open for travel\nD. The timeline of the Ice Age\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine when humans crossed into the Americas, anthropologists must rely on evidence regarding human presence in Siberia, the geological state of the Bering land bridge, and the climate-driven accessibility of migration routes during the Ice Age. The domestication of sled dogs is a separate cultural development that does not dictate the physical possibility or timing of the initial human migration. Therefore, it is the least relevant factor for establishing the timeline of the entry into the Americas.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
      "success": true,
