    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4846",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This question refers to the following information.\nAt last it seems to me that I have come to understand why man is the most fortunate of all creatures and consequently worthy of all admiration. … The nature of all other beings is limited. … Imagine! The great generosity of God! The happiness of man! To man it is allowed to be whatever he chooses to be!\nPico della Mirandola, Oration on the Dignity of Man, 1486\nFrom the passage, one may infer that Pico rejected the notion\n\nOptions: \nA. that man should not aspire to be whatever he chooses to be\nB. that God intended man to strive to achieve\nC. that all beings share the same nature\nD. that God is indifferent to man's happiness\nE. that humans are God's equals\nF. that man was a unique kind of creature\nG. that man has limited potential\nH. that man is unworthy of admiration\nI. that man has no control over his own destiny\nJ. that man is the least fortunate of all creatures\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The passage highlights that while \"the nature of all other beings is limited,\" man is uniquely granted the freedom to \"be whatever he chooses to be.\" By emphasizing this radical difference between human potential and the predetermined nature of other creatures, Pico rejects the notion that human nature is fixed or constrained. Therefore, he rejects the idea that man is subject to the same limited potential as other beings.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 298,
        "output_tokens": 1253,
        "total_tokens": 1551
      },
